   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Solving two-step equations\n- Two-step equations need two steps to solve.\n- Goal: get variable x alone on one side.\n- Undo operations with the opposite operation.\n- Layout: 2x + 3 = 11 / 3 added / subtract 3 both sides\n\n## Step 1: undo addition or subtraction\n- 3 is added in 2x + 3 = 11.\n- Subtract 3 from both sides.\n\n## Step 2: undo multiplication or division\n- x is multiplied by 2.\n- Divide both sides by 2.\n\n## Most important rule\n- Do the same to both sides.\n- The most important rule is that whatever you do to one side of the equation you must do to the other side.\n- An equation is like a balanced scale.\n- Subtracting 3 from one side only makes it untrue.\n\n## Check and second example\n- Always check in original: 2 times 4 plus 3 equals 11.\n- Left equals right; answer correct.\n- For 3x - 5 = 10: add 5 to both sides, get 3x = 15, divide both sides by 3, get x = 5.",
    "mirror": "## What a two-step equation is\n- A two-step equation needs two steps to solve, so the name tells you how many steps the working takes.\n- The goal is to get the variable x alone on one side of the equals sign, so the other numbers must be undone.\n- We undo the operations that have been done to x by using the opposite operation, so each step reverses one operation.\n\n## Undoing 2x + 3 = 11 one step at a time\n- In 2x + 3 = 11 the number 3 is added, so step one subtracts 3 from both sides and gives 2x = 8.\n- Here x is multiplied by 2, so step two divides both sides by 2 and gives x = 4.\n- Step one is to undo addition or subtraction, so the plus or minus is handled before the times or divide.\n- Step two is to undo multiplication or division, so the multiply or divide is undone after step one.\n- Layout: 2x + 3 = 11 → subtract 3 from both sides → 2x = 8 → divide both sides by 2 → x = 4.\n\n## The most important rule: both sides stay equal\n- If you subtract 3 from only one side, the equation is no longer true, so the same move must go on both sides.\n- An equation is like a balanced scale, so doing the same thing to both sides keeps it balanced.\n- The most important rule is that whatever you do to one side you must do to the other side, because that keeps it true.\n\n## Checking the answer, then a second example\n- For x = 4, 2 times 4 plus 3 is 8 plus 3, which is 11.\n- The left side equals the right side, so the answer is correct, because a check puts the answer back in.\n- Always check your answer by putting it back into the original equation, so you can see whether both sides match.\n- In 3x - 5 = 10, adding 5 to both sides gives 3x = 15, so dividing both sides by 3 gives x = 5.\n- Your turn: choose any two-step equation, undo the operations with the opposite operation, then check it by putting the answer back in."
